      "text": "# Community Garden Operating Agreement & Management Plan\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\nThis document establishes a transparent, inclusive, and financially sustainable framework for the community garden. It addresses the current resource constraints (30 plots for 40 households + 10 waitlisted), financial deficits, and social conflicts. The core philosophy is **\"Equity over Equality,\"** ensuring that physical ability, financial status, and time availability do not prevent participation.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Inclusive Operating Agreement\n\n### Article I: Membership & Allocation\n**Clause 1.1: Eligibility & Definition of Household**\nA \"Household\" is defined as one or more individuals sharing a primary residence. One plot is allocated per household. Single-person households are eligible for full plots but may opt for \"Shared Plots\" (see Clause 1.3).\n\n**Clause 1.2: Transparent Allocation Process**\nTo address the council\u2019s request for transparency, plots are allocated via a **Weighted Lottery System** rather than first-come-first-served.\n*   **Step 1:** All applicants (current members and waitlist) submit a registration form.\n*   **Step 2:** Points are assigned based on:\n    *   Residency within 1 mile of garden: +2 points\n    *   First-time applicant: +1 point\n    *   Household with children under 12: +1 point\n    *   Household with elderly member (65+): +1 point\n    *   Household with disability: +2 points (to prioritize accessible plots)\n*   **Step 3:** A public lottery is held annually. Top 30 point-holders receive plots. Remaining applicants enter the waitlist.\n\n**Clause 1.3: Plot Types & Accessibility**\n*   **Standard Plots (20 available):** 4x8 ft raised beds.\n*   **Accessible Plots (5 available):** 4x8 ft raised beds with 30-inch clearance underneath for wheelchair access; located near main paths.\n*   **Shared Plots (5 available):** Two households share one 4x8 ft bed. This allows smaller households or those with limited time to participate. Shared plots require a signed co-management agreement.\n\n**Clause 1.4: Waitlist Management**\n*   The waitlist is capped at 20 households.\n*   **Fair Handling:** Waitlist position is determined by the same weighted lottery points. If a plot becomes available mid-year, the next household on the waitlist is offered the plot. If they decline, the offer moves to the next.\n*   **Priority Re-entry:** Households that voluntarily give up a plot return to the *bottom* of the waitlist. Households removed for non-compliance return to the *bottom* of the waitlist.\n\n### Article II: Volunteering & Labor Equity\n**Clause 2.1: The \"Time Bank\" System**\nTo address the issue that \"some residents cannot perform physical work,\" the garden adopts a Time Bank model. Every household must contribute **4 hours per season** (spring/fall) or **2 hours per month** (summer) to common tasks.\n\n**Clause 2.2: Accessible Volunteering Roles**\nVolunteering is not limited to physical labor. Roles include:\n*   **Physical:** Path maintenance, compost turning, watering.\n*   **Non-Physical:**\n    *   *Garden Monitor:* Sitting at the entrance to greet visitors and enforce rules (seated role).\n    *   *Communications Lead:* Managing the newsletter, social media, and complaint log.\n    *   *Tool Librarian:* Checking tools in/out and maintaining inventory (light lifting only).\n    *   *Youth Mentor:* Supervising children\u2019s garden section (requires background check).\n    *   *Grant Writer:* Seeking external funding for equipment.\n\n**Clause 2.3: Exemption & Substitution**\nHouseholds unable to volunteer due to disability, illness, or lack of time may:\n1.  Pay a **Labor Buy-Out Fee** of $50/season (added to plot fee).\n2.  Arrange a substitute volunteer (must be registered).\n3.  Request a **Medical Exemption** (requires doctor\u2019s note; no fee, but household must attend 1 community meeting per year).\n\n### Article III: Conduct & Conflict Prevention\n**Clause 3.1: Chemical-Free Zone**\nThe garden is strictly organic. No synthetic pesticides, herbicides, or fungicides are permitted. Use of organic-approved sprays (e.g., neem oil) requires 24-hour notice to neighbors via the garden board.\n\n**Clause 3.2: Shade & Plant Height**\n*   Tall plants (corn, sunflowers, trellised beans) must be planted on the **north side** of the plot",
